    <section class="itbs-sec" id="itbs-what">
      <h2>What Is an Inverted Triangle Body Shape?</h2>
      <p>An inverted triangle body shape has a bust that's clearly wider than the hips, without the sharp waist taper you'd see in a top hourglass. It's the shape behind a strong, athletic-looking upper body with a narrower, straighter lower half. If you haven't measured yet, our full <a href="/">body type calculator</a> will tell you exactly where you land across all seven shapes.</p>
      <p>The formula: bust has to be at least 3.6 inches wider than the hips, and the gap between bust and waist has to stay under 9 inches. That second condition is what most people miss — an inverted triangle isn't waistless, it just doesn't have the dramatic 9-plus-inch taper that would push it into top hourglass territory instead.</p>
      <div class="itbs-note">
        <p><strong>Quick check:</strong> bust at least 3.6 inches wider than the hips, waist within 9 inches of the bust measurement. If your waist is more than 9 inches smaller than your bust, you're actually a top hourglass, not an inverted triangle — the styling shifts to favour showing off that waist instead of softening the shoulder line.</p>
      </div>
      <figure class="itbs-fig">
        <img src="/assets/images/inverted-triangle-body-shape-3d-front-side-back.webp" width="1500" height="1136" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="Inverted triangle body shape 3D illustration from the front, side and back, with bust, waist and hips measurement lines labelled at 40, 35 and 36 inches">
        <figcaption class="itbs-figcap">An inverted triangle body shape rendered in 3D from the measurements above: bust clearly wider than the hips, waist softly tapered. Open the <a href="/body-visualizer/">body visualizer</a> to rotate it yourself and try your own numbers.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p>You can preview this silhouette interactively in our <a href="/body-visualizer/">body simulator</a>: choose the female model, tap <strong>Inverted triangle</strong> under <em>Try a shape</em> and rotate the figure. Switch to the male model to see the same broad-shoulder pattern on a man.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="itbs-sec" id="itbs-common">
      <h2>Who Tends to Have This Shape?</h2>
      <p>It's especially common among competitive swimmers, and among anyone who trains the shoulders, lats and upper back heavily without matching lower-body volume — the upper-body width in this shape often comes from muscle as much as frame. It also occurs naturally in women with broader shoulders or a fuller bust and comparatively narrow hip bones, independent of training. Both routes land in the same measurement category.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="itbs-sec" id="itbs-compare">
      <h2>Inverted Triangle vs Top Hourglass — What's the Real Difference?</h2>
      <p>Both shapes have a bust wider than the hips, which is why they're constantly confused. The waist is what splits them.</p>
      <div class="itbs-tbl-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Trait</th><th scope="col">Inverted Triangle</th><th scope="col">Top Hourglass</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Bust vs. hips</td><td>3.6 in or more wider</td><td>1–10 in wider</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Waist definition</td><td>Under 9 in smaller than bust (soft)</td><td>9 in or more smaller than bust (sharp)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Visual read</td><td>Broad, straight line from shoulder to hip</td><td>Broad on top, but visibly nipped at the waist</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Styling focus</td><td>Add volume below, soften the shoulder line</td><td>Show the waist, balance is secondary</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <figure class="itbs-fig">
        <img src="/assets/images/inverted-triangle-vs-top-hourglass-body-shape-3d.webp" width="1120" height="1148" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="3D comparison of inverted triangle and top hourglass body shapes at the same hip size, with bust, waist and hips labelled">
        <figcaption class="itbs-figcap">Same broad bust and shoulders on both — the top hourglass simply has a much sharper waist cut-in.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p>If generic "broad shoulders" styling advice has felt slightly off, it's usually because it was written for a top hourglass — someone with the same shoulder width as you, but a much more defined waist to work with. Our full <a href="/">body type calculator</a> tells you which of the two you actually are.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="itbs-sec" id="itbs-glance">
      <h2>Styling an Inverted Triangle at a Glance</h2>
      <p>The whole strategy is a transfer of visual weight: take some away from the shoulders, add some to the hips and legs. Here is how that plays out category by category — the full reasoning, with 3D comparisons, is further down.</p>
      <div class="itbs-tbl-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Category</th><th scope="col">Adds balance below — choose</th><th scope="col">Adds width on top — skip</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Necklines</td><td>Deep V, scoop, halter</td><td>Boat, off-shoulder, wide square</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Sleeves</td><td>Raglan, fitted, sleeveless</td><td>Puff, dropped shoulder, epaulettes</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Tops &amp; knits</td><td>Soft drape, fine-gauge V-neck</td><td>Boxy, chunky, shoulder-padded</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Outerwear</td><td>Soft longline, raglan A-line coat</td><td>Strong-shoulder blazer, caped coat</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Trousers &amp; jeans</td><td>Wide-leg, flared, bootcut</td><td>Skinny, tapered</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Skirts &amp; dresses</td><td>Full, A-line, fit-and-flare</td><td>Pencil, straight shift</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Shorts &amp; jumpsuits</td><td>Culotte shorts, halter wide-leg</td><td>Slim shorts, puff-sleeve skinny</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="itbs-sec" id="itbs-whr">
      <h2>The Waist-to-Hip Ratio Quirk for This Shape</h2>
      <p>Waist-to-hip ratio (WHR) can be misleading for an inverted triangle, and it's worth understanding why. Because hips are the smaller of your two key measurements in this shape, WHR — waist divided by hip — often comes out higher than it would for a pear or hourglass shape with the same waist size, sometimes landing at 0.85 or above simply because the hip number in the denominator is small.</p>
      <p>WHR was designed around variable fat distribution, not variable hip-bone width. A narrow-hipped, broad-shouldered frame can post a WHR that looks elevated on paper without carrying the same abdominal-fat pattern that actually drives risk in an apple shape. If your WHR reads high here, waist-to-height ratio (WHtR) is the more reliable second check, since it only uses your waist and height — no hip measurement involved at all.</p>
      <p>Run both on our <a href="/waist-to-hip-ratio-calculator/">waist to hip ratio calculator</a> and <a href="/waist-to-height-ratio-calculator/">waist to height ratio calculator</a> to see how the two compare for your own numbers. For the shoulder-to-waist ratio and other proportions specific to this build, use the <a href="/body-proportions-calculator/">body proportions calculator</a>.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="itbs-sec" id="itbs-style">
      <h2>How to Dress for an Inverted Triangle Body Shape</h2>
      <p>The core rule for this shape is the mirror image of a pear’s: softening the shoulder line rather than reinforcing it, and adding volume through the lower body to balance it. The sections below work through each category piece by piece — rendered as 3D side-by-sides on an inverted-triangle figure (40–33–36 in) with broad shoulders, so you can see how each cut shifts the balance between top and bottom.</p>

      <h3 id="inverted-triangle-necklines">Necklines</h3>
      <p>A neckline that opens up <strong>vertically rather than horizontally</strong> is the single most useful adjustment available here, because it draws a line down the centre of the body instead of adding emphasis along the shoulder seam. A <strong>V-neckline</strong>, especially a deeper one, does this most clearly. A plain scoop neck does a softer version of the same thing. Boat necklines and off-the-shoulder styles are the ones to be selective with — both run in a wide horizontal line right along the shoulder, which is exactly the measurement you’re usually trying to soften rather than emphasise further.</p>
      <figure class="itbs-gfig">
        <img src="/assets/images/3d-guide/inverted-triangle-necklines-3d.webp" width="1200" height="1000" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="3D comparison of necklines on an inverted triangle body shape — avoid: off-shoulder or boat neck (draws a line straight across the broadest point); choose: deep v or scoop (a vertical opening narrows the upper body)">
      </figure>

      <h3 id="inverted-triangle-sleeves">Sleeves</h3>
      <p>Sleeve construction changes where the shoulder seam actually sits, which matters more here than almost any other detail. A <strong>raglan sleeve</strong>, which runs in a diagonal line from the collar to the underarm instead of a horizontal seam at the shoulder tip, softens the shoulder point noticeably. A simple fitted sleeve with no shoulder padding or structure does the same job. Puff sleeves, dropped shoulders, and anything with shoulder padding are the ones to limit — all three add width or emphasis at the exact point that’s already the widest part of the frame.</p>
      <figure class="itbs-gfig">
        <img src="/assets/images/3d-guide/inverted-triangle-sleeves-3d.webp" width="1200" height="1000" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="3D comparison of sleeves on an inverted triangle body shape — avoid: puff sleeve (adds width right where you are broadest); choose: raglan or fitted sleeve (follows the arm with no extra shoulder bulk)">
      </figure>

      <h3 id="inverted-triangle-shirts-t-shirts-and-tops">Shirts, T-Shirts &amp; Tops</h3>
      <p>Soft, fluid fabric does more for this shape than structure does. A <strong>draped or wrap top</strong> in a fabric with some movement — jersey, a soft crepe, a fluid viscose — skims over the shoulder rather than defining it sharply, which takes some of the visual weight out of the upper body. A simple V-neck top in a soft fabric does the same job with less effort. Structured, boxy, or heavily tailored tops are worth being selective with, since crisp fabric and a defined shoulder seam both add sharpness and width to an area that’s usually already the broadest point of the silhouette.</p>
      <figure class="itbs-gfig">
        <img src="/assets/images/3d-guide/inverted-triangle-tops-3d.webp" width="1200" height="1000" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="3D comparison of tops on an inverted triangle body shape — avoid: structured, boxy top (stiff fabric squares off the shoulder even more); choose: soft draped v-neck top (skims the shoulder and softens the line)">
      </figure>

      <h3 id="inverted-triangle-knitwear">Knitwear</h3>
      <p>A <strong>fitted, fine-gauge sweater</strong> in a V-neck or scoop keeps the upper body looking streamlined rather than adding bulk on top of an already broad shoulder line. Lightweight yarn matters here as much as the cut does — a thin, closely fitted knit sits close to the body instead of standing away from it. Chunky, cable-knit, or boxy sweaters are the harder choice: thick yarn adds visible volume at the shoulder and bust, and a boxy cut adds structure on top of that, both compounding a proportion that’s usually already working against you.</p>
      <figure class="itbs-gfig">
        <img src="/assets/images/3d-guide/inverted-triangle-knitwear-3d.webp" width="1200" height="1000" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="3D comparison of knitwear on an inverted triangle body shape — avoid: chunky, shoulder-padded knit (bulks up shoulders and bust together); choose: fine-knit v-neck (keeps the upper body streamlined)">
      </figure>

      <h3 id="inverted-triangle-jackets-and-blazers">Jackets &amp; Blazers</h3>
      <p>An <strong>unstructured, longline cardigan-jacket</strong> or a soft duster coat adds length to the silhouette without adding any width at the shoulder — it hangs from the body rather than being built out from it. A wrap coat with a tie belt does something similar while adding a defined waist. Structured blazers with strong shoulders, padding, or a sharp lapel are the ones to be careful with: tailoring in that category is specifically designed to build the shoulder line out and square it off, which is the opposite of what usually helps this shape.</p>
      <figure class="itbs-gfig">
        <img src="/assets/images/3d-guide/inverted-triangle-jackets-3d.webp" width="1200" height="1000" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="3D comparison of jackets on an inverted triangle body shape — avoid: strong-shoulder blazer (padding and seams reinforce the shoulder line); choose: soft, unstructured longline (adds length instead of width)">
      </figure>
      <h3 id="inverted-triangle-coats">Coats</h3>
      <p>Move the coat's volume from the shoulder to the hem. Capes, strong epaulettes and wide peak lapels stack more width onto the broadest line of your body and leave the hips looking narrower still. A <strong>raglan-sleeve coat</strong> removes the shoulder seam altogether, and a gentle A-line cut adds a little width below the waist, so the silhouette evens out from top to bottom. Single-breasted and knee-length is the easiest version to wear; a wrap coat with a tie belt is a good alternative if you want a defined waist.</p>
      <figure class="itbs-gfig">
        <img src="/assets/images/3d-guide/inverted-triangle-coats-3d.webp" width="1200" height="1000" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="3D comparison of coats on an inverted triangle body shape — avoid: caped or big-shoulder coat (widens the top and leaves the hips narrow); choose: raglan-sleeve a-line coat (soft shoulder, gentle flare toward the hem)">
      </figure>

      <h3 id="inverted-triangle-trousers">Trousers</h3>
      <p><strong>Wide-leg and bootcut trousers</strong> add volume at the bottom of the silhouette, which does the balancing work that a narrower leg can’t — the goal is a lower body with enough visual weight to hold its own against broader shoulders above it. A relaxed straight-leg cut is a slightly more streamlined version of the same idea. Skinny or tightly tapered jeans are the more difficult choice here, since a narrow leg leaves the bottom half of the silhouette with very little visual weight, which makes the shoulder-to-hip imbalance more obvious rather than less.</p>
      <figure class="itbs-gfig">
        <img src="/assets/images/3d-guide/inverted-triangle-trousers-3d.webp" width="1200" height="1000" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="3D comparison of trousers on an inverted triangle body shape — avoid: skinny trousers (leaves nothing below to balance the top); choose: wide-leg trousers (adds volume below to balance the shoulders)">
      </figure>
      <h3 id="inverted-triangle-jeans">Jeans</h3>
      <p>This is the shape that can wear a real flare. <strong>Flared or wide-leg jeans</strong> add width at the ankle that mirrors the width at the shoulder, turning a V-shaped silhouette into something closer to an X. Details that add interest at the hip — flap back pockets, a lighter wash, contrast stitching — help as well. Skinny jeans leave the body tapering in one direction from shoulder to ankle, which is exactly the pattern that makes the upper body look heavier than it is.</p>
      <figure class="itbs-gfig">
        <img src="/assets/images/3d-guide/inverted-triangle-jeans-3d.webp" width="1200" height="1000" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="3D comparison of jeans on an inverted triangle body shape — avoid: skinny jeans (the body tapers from shoulder to ankle); choose: flared jeans (width at the hem mirrors the shoulders)">
      </figure>

      <h3 id="inverted-triangle-skirts">Skirts</h3>
      <p>A skirt with some built-in volume — a <strong>full skirt, an A-line, or anything with soft pleating</strong> — gives the lower half of the silhouette enough visual weight to balance a broader shoulder line above it. A skirt that flares from the waist rather than skimming straight down does this most clearly. A straight or pencil-cut skirt is the harder choice in this category: it keeps the lower body narrow and close to the leg line, which leaves the shoulder-to-hip proportion looking more lopsided than a fuller shape below the waist would.</p>
      <figure class="itbs-gfig">
        <img src="/assets/images/3d-guide/inverted-triangle-skirts-3d.webp" width="1200" height="1000" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="3D comparison of skirts on an inverted triangle body shape — avoid: straight pencil skirt (keeps the lower half narrow); choose: full or a-line skirt (builds width below to meet the shoulders)">
      </figure>

      <h3 id="inverted-triangle-dresses">Dresses</h3>
      <p>A <strong>fit-and-flare dress</strong> nips in at the waist and then flares outward, which gives the lower half of the silhouette real volume to work with against a broader shoulder line — the flare is doing the same balancing job that a full skirt or wide-leg trouser does elsewhere in this guide. An A-line dress with some movement in the skirt achieves something similar with a softer transition at the waist. A straight shift dress is the more difficult choice: it runs in one continuous line from shoulder to hem with no added volume below, so the shoulder stays the widest point of the whole silhouette.</p>
      <figure class="itbs-gfig">
        <img src="/assets/images/3d-guide/inverted-triangle-dresses-3d.webp" width="1200" height="1000" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="3D comparison of dresses on an inverted triangle body shape — avoid: straight shift dress (one column from shoulder to hem); choose: v-neck fit-and-flare (narrows above, flares below the waist)">
      </figure>

      <h3 id="inverted-triangle-shorts">Shorts</h3>
      <p><strong>Wide-leg or culotte-style shorts</strong> extend the same balancing logic used elsewhere on this page into warm-weather dressing — a bit of flare and movement in the leg gives the lower body some visual weight rather than leaving it narrow underneath broader shoulders. A relaxed, slightly cropped wide leg keeps this practical for everyday wear. Slim, tailored shorts with a narrow leg are the harder choice: they keep the lower body streamlined and close-fitting, which does nothing to offset a shoulder line that’s usually already the widest point of the outfit.</p>
      <figure class="itbs-gfig">
        <img src="/assets/images/3d-guide/inverted-triangle-shorts-3d.webp" width="1200" height="1000" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="3D comparison of shorts on an inverted triangle body shape — avoid: slim, tailored shorts (adds no volume to balance the top); choose: wide-leg culotte shorts (widens the lower half)">
      </figure>
      <h3 id="inverted-triangle-jumpsuits-and-playsuits">Jumpsuits &amp; Playsuits</h3>
      <p>A <strong>halter neckline</strong> is one of the best tops this shape can wear: the straps cut in toward the neck and leave the shoulder point bare, which visually narrows it. On a jumpsuit, pair that with a wide leg and you get volume exactly where you need it. V-neck and wrap-front styles do the same job with more coverage. The combination to skip is puff sleeves or a boat neck on a skinny-leg jumpsuit — every bit of volume sits at the top.</p>
      <figure class="itbs-gfig">
        <img src="/assets/images/3d-guide/inverted-triangle-jumpsuits-3d.webp" width="1200" height="1000" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="3D comparison of jumpsuits on an inverted triangle body shape — avoid: puff-sleeve skinny jumpsuit (all the volume sits at the shoulder); choose: halter wide-leg jumpsuit (cuts in at the shoulder, flares at the hem)">
      </figure>

    </section>

    <section class="itbs-sec" id="itbs-measure">
      <h2>Common Inverted Triangle Measurements by Size</h2>
      <p>The formula holds at every size — bust noticeably wider than hips, with a waist that stays fairly close to the bust:</p>
